  "worked_solution": "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cot}\u2061(2\\pi t)),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{2}\u2061(2\\pi t))\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-4\\pi {csc}^{2}\u2061(2\\pi t),4\\pi \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2\\pi t)\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2\\pi t)\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=\\frac{1}{8},$ we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{1}{8}) & =\u27e8-4\\pi {csc}^{2}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),4\\pi \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-4\\pi (\\sqrt{2})^{2},4\\pi (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-4\\pi (2),4\\pi (\\frac{1}{2})\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-8\\pi ,\\,2\\pi \u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
